      "cultural_notes_vi": "Restaurant Hàn culture: (1) NO TIP — không cần tip, không expected; tip = bị từ chối hoặc awkward; (2) Service charge KHÔNG có ở Hàn restaurants typical — nếu thấy 'service charge' trên bill, có thể là scam targeting tourist (hỏi policy ngay); (3) Banchan (반찬, side dishes) FREE refill — staff sẽ refill mà không tính tiền; (4) Splitting bill (더치페이) culturally OK với bạn nhưng business meal = host trả; (5) Dispute resolution: staff thường offer free dessert/drink/discount nếu issue confirmed; (6) Ratings system: Naver review + Google review quan trọng — 1 review tệ ảnh hưởng business 1 tháng. Quy tắc khiếu nại tourist: (a) Take photo of dish + menu page BEFORE eating (evidence); (b) Save bill receipt; (c) Khiếu nại CỤ THỂ ('không cay nhưng ra cay' > 'không ngon'); (d) Đề xuất giải pháp ('exchange') không ultimatum ('refund or I leave'); (e) Nếu không resolve → review honest factual trên Naver/Google + share photos. Mẹo: tourist trap restaurants ở Myeongdong & Itaewon hay charge extra 'service' — local restaurants không bao giờ. Avoid quán có tiếng Anh menu + 'WELCOME TOURIST' sign — usually overpriced. Tốt hơn: quán có chỉ Hàn menu + đông khách Hàn = authentic + fair price.",
      "cultural_notes_en": "Korean restaurant culture: (1) NO TIP — not needed, not expected; tipping = refused or awkward; (2) a service charge is NOT standard at typical Korean restaurants — if you see a 'service charge' on the bill, it may be a scam targeting tourists (ask about the policy right away); (3) banchan (반찬, side dishes) are FREE refills — staff refill them at no charge; (4) splitting the bill (더치페이) is culturally fine with friends, but a business meal = the host pays; (5) dispute resolution: staff usually offer a free dessert/drink/discount if the issue is confirmed; (6) the ratings system: Naver + Google reviews matter — one bad review can hurt a business for a month. Tourist complaint rules: (a) take a photo of the dish + menu page BEFORE eating (evidence); (b) save the bill receipt; (c) complain SPECIFICALLY ('it wasn't supposed to be spicy but it came spicy' > 'it's not tasty'); (d) propose a solution ('an exchange') not an ultimatum ('refund or I leave'); (e) if unresolved → an honest factual review on Naver/Google + share photos. Tip: tourist-trap restaurants in Myeongdong & Itaewon often charge an extra 'service' fee — local restaurants never do. Avoid places with an English menu + a 'WELCOME TOURIST' sign — usually overpriced. Better: a place with a Korean-only menu + lots of Korean customers = authentic + fair price.",
      "tip_advice_vi": "Khi gặp problem ở restaurant Hàn: (1) DON'T touch food much trước khi báo — eaten 1 bite then complain = lose credibility ('chắc bạn ăn rồi mới phát hiện không hợp khẩu vị'); (2) RAISE issue NGAY khi món ra — wait too long = staff khó xử; (3) USE soft language: '~ 것 같아요' (seems like) > '~ 이에요' (it is); (4) PROPOSE solution: '교환해 주세요' (exchange please) > '환불해 주세요' (refund please) — exchange easier for staff; (5) STAY CALM — Hàn customer service responds best to calm specific complaints. Bill problem: (a) Check itemized bill before paying — Hàn bills detailed; (b) If unfamiliar charge: '이 항목이 뭐예요?' (what's this item?); (c) Service charge surprise: '메뉴에 서비스 차지 표시 없었는데요?' (service charge wasn't shown on menu) — usually they remove it; (d) Card payment: ALWAYS get receipt — tax record. Sau visit: nếu service tốt despite issue, leave compliment review. Nếu service tệ, leave factual review (KHÔNG emotional). Hàn restaurant industry rất sensitive với reviews — staff bị ảnh hưởng compensation.",
      "tip_advice_en": "When you hit a problem at a Korean restaurant: (1) DON'T eat much of the food before reporting — having eaten a bite then complaining = lost credibility ('you probably ate it then decided it didn't suit you'); (2) RAISE the issue RIGHT when the dish arrives — waiting too long = staff struggle to address it; (3) USE soft language: '~ 것 같아요' (it seems like) > '~ 이에요' (it is); (4) PROPOSE a solution: '교환해 주세요' (please exchange it) > '환불해 주세요' (please refund it) — an exchange is easier for staff; (5) STAY CALM — Korean customer service responds best to calm, specific complaints. Bill problem: (a) check the itemized bill before paying — Korean bills are detailed; (b) for an unfamiliar charge: '이 항목이 뭐예요?' (what's this item?); (c) a surprise service charge: '메뉴에 서비스 차지 표시 없었는데요?' (the service charge wasn't shown on the menu) — they usually remove it; (d) card payment: ALWAYS get a receipt — a tax record. After the visit: if service was good despite the issue, leave a compliment review. If service was bad, leave a factual review (NOT emotional). The Korean restaurant industry is very sensitive to reviews — staff compensation is affected.",
