      "cultural_notes_vi": "시부모님 dynamics ở Hàn rất khác Việt Nam: (1) Truyền thống Hàn — vợ visits husband's family thường xuyên hơn vợ Việt visits chồng. Lễ tết (명절) — daughter-in-law thường về nhà husband's family TRƯỚC, sau đó mới về nhà mình; modern Hàn shift towards equal time nhưng older gen vẫn expects traditional. (2) Cooking — daughter-in-law thường được expect help với 차례 (ancestor rites cooking) at Chuseok/Seollal. Vietnamese wife với non-existent Korean cooking experience = stressful first year. (3) Communication — Korean in-laws RARELY criticize directly. Tín hiệu indirect: silence at dinner, pointed comments to spouse, suggestions framed as 'modern young people' generalization. Decoding signals critical. (4) 정 (jeong — emotional bond) takes years to build, but once built, in-laws become extremely supportive. First 2-3 years = test period. Vietnamese wife strategy: high effort first 2 years (visit often, learn cooking basics, maintain formality), gradual relax phase 3+. (5) Quy tắc vàng: when in doubt about register/formality, default to MORE formal — Hàn appreciate respect even when unnecessary, dislike too-casual. (6) Modern shift: 30-40% younger Korean families are more flexible, especially urban Seoul. Older / rural / wealthy traditional families more conservative. Read your specific in-laws, not stereotype.",
      "cultural_notes_en": "시부모님 (in-law) dynamics in Korea differ greatly from Vietnam: (1) Korean tradition — a wife visits the husband's family more often than a Vietnamese wife visits the husband's. At festivals (명절), the daughter-in-law usually goes to the husband's family FIRST, then her own; modern Korea is shifting toward equal time but the older generation still expects the traditional pattern. (2) Cooking — the daughter-in-law is usually expected to help with 차례 (ancestor-rite cooking) at Chuseok/Seollal. A Vietnamese wife with no Korean cooking experience = a stressful first year. (3) Communication — Korean in-laws RARELY criticize directly. Indirect signals: silence at dinner, pointed comments to the spouse, suggestions framed as 'modern young people' generalizations. Decoding the signals is critical. (4) 정 (jeong — the emotional bond) takes years to build, but once built, in-laws become extremely supportive. The first 2–3 years = a test period. Vietnamese-wife strategy: high effort the first 2 years (visit often, learn cooking basics, keep formality), gradual relaxing from year 3+. (5) Golden rule: when in doubt about register/formality, default to MORE formal — Koreans appreciate respect even when unnecessary, and dislike too-casual. (6) Modern shift: 30–40% of younger Korean families are more flexible, especially urban Seoul. Older / rural / wealthy-traditional families are more conservative. Read your specific in-laws, not the stereotype.",
      "tip_advice_vi": "Five-step approach cho difficult conversation với 시부모님: (1) NEVER spontaneous — request a quiet moment '잠시 시간 괜찮으세요?'. Không bao giờ raise sensitive topic during meal hoặc family event; (2) HUSBAND/WIFE alignment trước — discuss với spouse, agree position, decide who speaks. Hàn families expect SPOUSE to be mouthpiece, không direct in-law-to-in-law; (3) FORMAL OPENING — '죄송합니다만, 한 가지 말씀드리고 싶은 게 있습니다'. Frames như humble request, không demand; (4) ACKNOWLEDGE traditional view trước — '가풍을 존중합니다만…'; (5) CONCRETE proposal — không generic 'I want flexibility', mà specific 'Em xin về Việt Nam mùng 3 Tết, 1 năm 1 lần'. Concrete = easier to negotiate. AVOID: comparison Việt vs Hàn ('ở Việt Nam thì…'), criticism of Korean traditions, emotional language. Sau cuộc nói chuyện: gửi 1 hộp trái cây hoặc bánh nhỏ tới 시댁 trong tuần đó — gesture of respect that softens any tension. Continued effort over time builds 정.",
      "tip_advice_en": "A five-step approach for a difficult conversation with 시부모님: (1) NEVER spontaneous — request a quiet moment, '잠시 시간 괜찮으세요?'. Never raise a sensitive topic during a meal or family event; (2) HUSBAND/WIFE alignment first — discuss with your spouse, agree on a position, decide who speaks. Korean families expect the SPOUSE to be the mouthpiece, not direct in-law-to-in-law; (3) FORMAL OPENING — '죄송합니다만, 한 가지 말씀드리고 싶은 게 있습니다'. Frame it as a humble request, not a demand; (4) ACKNOWLEDGE the traditional view first — '가풍을 존중합니다만…' (I respect the family customs, but…); (5) A CONCRETE proposal — not a generic 'I want flexibility', but a specific 'I'd like to go back to Vietnam on the 3rd day of Tết, once a year'. Concrete = easier to negotiate. AVOID: a Vietnam-vs-Korea comparison ('in Vietnam we…'), criticism of Korean traditions, emotional language. After the conversation: send a box of fruit or small pastries to the 시댁 (in-laws' home) that week — a gesture of respect that softens any tension. Sustained effort over time builds 정.",
