      "cultural_notes_vi": "Friendship + politics ở Hàn — younger generation (20-30s) more open thảo luận chính trị, older generation tends to avoid trong xã giao. Topic phổ biến nhạy: (1) NK/SK — đa số người Hàn miền Nam ÚNG HỘ thống nhất nhưng KHÔNG ủng hộ regime Bắc; tránh oversimplify. (2) Japan — colonial history (1910-1945) vẫn emotional; gen Z bắt đầu less invested. (3) Gender politics — feminism 4B movement (no dating, sex, marriage, children) controversial; nam giới Hàn trẻ cảm thấy bị tấn công. (4) Wealth gap — 강남 vs khu khác, '금수저' (gold spoon, born rich) vs '흙수저' (dirt spoon, born poor). (5) Education — Suneung pressure, 학원 (cram schools) culture. Với bạn Việt: bạn Hàn có thể assume Việt Nam tương tự các nước Á khác trên topic này — gentle correction OK. Tránh: cáu, raise voice, declarations 'Vietnam X is better/worse than Korea Y'. Goal: thảo luận để hiểu, không để thắng. Sau heated topic: chuyển sang light topic 5-10 phút, signaling friendship still intact.",
      "cultural_notes_en": "Friendship + politics in Korea — the younger generation (20s–30s) is more open to discussing politics; the older generation tends to avoid it socially. Common sensitive topics: (1) NK/SK — most South Koreans SUPPORT reunification but do NOT support the Northern regime; avoid oversimplifying. (2) Japan — colonial history (1910–1945) is still emotional; Gen Z is starting to be less invested. (3) Gender politics — the 4B feminism movement (no dating, sex, marriage, children) is controversial; young Korean men feel attacked. (4) The wealth gap — Gangnam vs other districts, '금수저' (gold spoon, born rich) vs '흙수저' (dirt spoon, born poor). (5) Education — Suneung pressure, 학원 (cram school) culture. With a Vietnamese friend: your Korean friend may assume Vietnam is similar to other Asian countries on these topics — a gentle correction is OK. Avoid: getting angry, raising your voice, declarations like 'Vietnam X is better/worse than Korea Y'. The goal: discuss to understand, not to win. After a heated topic: switch to a light topic for 5–10 minutes, signaling the friendship is still intact.",
      "tip_advice_vi": "Survival kit cho political conversation với bạn Hàn: (1) PAUSE 3 giây trước khi answer — Hàn coi pause là dấu hiệu suy nghĩ, không phải bí. (2) Lead with PRINCIPLE không OPINION — 'em tin tôn trọng đa dạng quan điểm là quan trọng' tốt hơn 'em nghĩ X sai'. (3) ATTRIBUTE views thay vì own them — 'theo Việt Nam thường…', 'thế hệ em thường nghĩ…' — softens stance. (4) ASK questions TO LEARN — '한국 사람 입장에서 왜 그렇게 생각하세요?' (vì sao người Hàn nghĩ vậy?). Câu hỏi mở = không đối đầu. (5) PIVOT khi heated — ASK THEM về non-political topic. Bạn Hàn appreciate the graceful exit. Quy tắc 'agree to disagree': Hàn culture tôn trọng người giữ quan điểm consistent + respectful. Nếu cuộc nói chuyện turn ugly: '오늘 주제가 좀 무거운 거 같아요, 다음에 가벼운 거 마시면서 얘기할까요?' = 'today's topic seems heavy, next time over a light drink?' Resets relationship. KHÔNG: ghost bạn sau political fight — Hàn coi friendships 'survive' arguments là dấu hiệu real friendship. Chủ động reach out 1 tuần sau.",
      "tip_advice_en": "A survival kit for political conversation with a Korean friend: (1) PAUSE 3 seconds before answering — Koreans read a pause as a sign of thought, not of being stuck. (2) Lead with a PRINCIPLE not an OPINION — 'I believe respecting diverse views matters' beats 'I think X is wrong'. (3) ATTRIBUTE views rather than own them — 'in Vietnam people usually…', 'my generation tends to think…' — softens the stance. (4) ASK questions TO LEARN — '한국 사람 입장에서 왜 그렇게 생각하세요?' (why do Koreans think that way?). An open question = not confrontational. (5) PIVOT when it heats up — ASK THEM about a non-political topic. The Korean friend appreciates the graceful exit. The 'agree to disagree' rule: Korean culture respects someone who holds a consistent + respectful position. If the conversation turns ugly: '오늘 주제가 좀 무거운 거 같아요, 다음에 가벼운 거 마시면서 얘기할까요?' = 'today's topic feels heavy — next time over a light drink?' This resets the relationship. Do NOT ghost a friend after a political fight — Koreans see friendships that 'survive' arguments as a sign of real friendship. Proactively reach out a week later.",
