      "cultural_notes_vi": "Korean 장례 (funeral) culture: (1) Length — typically 3 days ('삼일장' Korean tradition); families gather at 장례식장 (funeral hall). (2) Visiting (조문) — go in person if possible, even briefly. Wear black suit / dark muted clothes. Bring 부의금 (condolence money) trong white envelope, written name on back. Số tiền: 50,000원 close acquaintance, 100,000원 friend, 200-300,000원 close friend. (3) Tại funeral: cúi chào portrait of deceased (incense optional), cúi chào family members (don't shake hand). Stay briefly (~10-20 phút), không lingering. (4) Conversation: '얼마나 마음이 아프시겠습니까' (lòng bạn chắc đau lắm) là standard. Lắng nghe nhiều hơn nói. Hỏi family có cần help practical (food, ride). DON'T hỏi cause of death. (5) Sau funeral: gửi simple message sau 1 tuần ('잘 보내드렸어요?') — indicates ongoing support. 49일째 (49th day) traditionally significant in Buddhist tradition; non-Buddhist families may skip. (6) Korean grieving differs từ Việt: less verbal expression of grief in public, more inner. Vietnamese friend should READ pause/silence as grief, không as awkwardness. (7) 정 (jeong) plays role: friends who show up at funeral are remembered FOR YEARS — long-term relationship investment moment.",
      "cultural_notes_en": "Korean 장례 (funeral) culture: (1) Length — typically 3 days ('삼일장', the Korean tradition); families gather at a 장례식장 (funeral hall). (2) Visiting (조문) — go in person if possible, even briefly. Wear a black suit / dark muted clothes. Bring 부의금 (condolence money) in a white envelope, your name written on the back. Amounts: ₩50,000 acquaintance, ₩100,000 friend, ₩200–300,000 close friend. (3) At the funeral: bow to the portrait of the deceased (incense optional), bow to the family members (don't shake hands). Stay briefly (~10–20 min), no lingering. (4) Conversation: '얼마나 마음이 아프시겠습니까' (your heart must ache so much) is standard. Listen more than you speak. Ask if the family needs practical help (food, a ride). DON'T ask the cause of death. (5) After the funeral: send a simple message after a week ('잘 보내드렸어요?') — indicating ongoing support. The 49일째 (49th day) is traditionally significant in the Buddhist tradition; non-Buddhist families may skip it. (6) Korean grieving differs from Vietnamese: less verbal expression of grief in public, more internal. A Vietnamese friend should READ pause/silence as grief, NOT as awkwardness. (7) 정 (jeong) plays a role: friends who show up at a funeral are remembered FOR YEARS — a long-term relationship-investment moment.",
      "tip_advice_vi": "Ba quy tắc vàng cho condolence: (1) PRESENCE > WORDS — đến (visit) là quan trọng nhất, lời nói thứ hai. Just sitting với friend in silence is comfort. (2) VALIDATE FEELINGS không try fix — '마음이 많이 아프시겠어요' (lòng bạn chắc đau lắm) > '괜찮아질 거예요' (sẽ ổn thôi). (3) ONGOING SUPPORT — không chỉ 1 lần ngày funeral. Reach out 1 tuần, 1 tháng, 6 tháng sau ('요즘 어때요?'). Korean grief is private nhưng appreciate sustained care. Specific actions: (a) Bring food (Korean culture: home-cooked or store-bought soup, prepared food — they don't have energy to cook); (b) Offer practical help (ride, pickup kids, errands); (c) DON'T post on social media về their loss (unless họ post first). What to AVOID: '시간이 지나면 좋아져요' (thời gian sẽ chữa — true but premature in first weeks); '하늘에서 잘 보고 계실 거예요' (họ đang nhìn từ trên trời — religious assumption); '그래도 ~한 게 다행이에요' (ít ra X là may — minimizing); 'I lost my X too' (centering yourself). Sau ~1 tháng: bắt đầu invite to light activities ('산책 갈래요?', 'cafe 갈래요?') — gentle pull back to life. Không expect them to act 'normal' for 6+ months.",
      "tip_advice_en": "Three golden rules for condolences: (1) PRESENCE > WORDS — showing up (visiting) matters most, words second. Just sitting with the friend in silence is comfort. (2) VALIDATE feelings, don't try to fix — '마음이 많이 아프시겠어요' (your heart must ache so much) > '괜찮아질 거예요' (it'll be okay). (3) ONGOING SUPPORT — not just once on the funeral day. Reach out at 1 week, 1 month, 6 months ('요즘 어때요?'). Korean grief is private but they appreciate sustained care. Specific actions: (a) bring food (Korean culture: home-cooked or store-bought soup, prepared food — they don't have energy to cook); (b) offer practical help (a ride, picking up kids, errands); (c) DON'T post on social media about their loss (unless they post first). What to AVOID: '시간이 지나면 좋아져요' (time heals — true but premature in the first weeks); '하늘에서 잘 보고 계실 거예요' (they're watching from heaven — a religious assumption); '그래도 ~한 게 다행이에요' (at least X is a relief — minimizing); 'I lost my X too' (centering yourself). After ~1 month: start inviting them to light activities ('산책 갈래요?' a walk?, 'cafe 갈래요?') — a gentle pull back to life. Don't expect them to act 'normal' for 6+ months.",
