      "cultural_notes_vi": "Apology culture Hàn 3-tier: (1) 즉시 사과 (immediate apology) — cho lỗi nhỏ tại chỗ, '죄송합니다' khi bumping into someone; (2) 정식 사과 (formal apology) — cho lỗi trung bình+, separate moment, face-to-face preferred; (3) 공식 사과 (public apology) — cho serious public/work issues. Quy tắc CRITICAL với Vietnamese learners: Hàn coi REGRET expressed thẳng = trustworthiness, KHÔNG weakness. Việt văn hóa đôi khi save face bằng cách downplay ('không có gì đâu') — Hàn coi là không thật. Sai lầm thường gặp: (a) Apology kèm explanation ('근데 제가 그날 너무 바빠서…') — bị coi là evading; (b) Apology generic ('미안해 다 미안해') — không acknowledged specific harm = không believable; (c) Move on quá nhanh — không cho Hàn person cơ hội bày tỏ feelings; (d) Materiel comp instead of verbal acknowledgement (gift first thay vì sorry first) — coi là buying off. Apology done well builds trust LONG-TERM. Hàn culture: friends/partners who can apologize cleanly = friends/partners worth keeping. The act ITSELF is positive moment, không negative. Sau apology accepted, Hàn LITERALLY move on — không bring up again. Nếu họ bring up lại = chưa fully accepted, cần additional repair work.",
      "cultural_notes_en": "Korean apology culture is 3-tier: (1) 즉시 사과 (immediate apology) — for a small on-the-spot slip, '죄송합니다' when bumping into someone; (2) 정식 사과 (formal apology) — for a moderate+ mistake, a separate moment, face-to-face preferred; (3) 공식 사과 (public apology) — for serious public/work issues. A CRITICAL rule for Vietnamese learners: Koreans see regret expressed plainly as trustworthiness, NOT weakness. Vietnamese culture sometimes saves face by downplaying ('it's nothing') — Koreans read this as insincere. Common mistakes: (a) an apology with an explanation attached ('근데 제가 그날 너무 바빠서…' — but I was so busy that day) — read as evading; (b) a generic apology ('미안해 다 미안해' — sorry, sorry for everything) — not acknowledging the specific harm = not believable; (c) moving on too fast — not giving the Korean person a chance to express their feelings; (d) material compensation instead of verbal acknowledgment (a gift first instead of sorry first) — read as buying it off. An apology done well builds trust LONG-TERM. Korean culture: friends/partners who can apologize cleanly = worth keeping. The act ITSELF is a positive moment, not a negative one. Once an apology is accepted, Koreans LITERALLY move on — they don't bring it up again. If they bring it up again = it wasn't fully accepted; more repair work is needed.",
      "tip_advice_vi": "Six-step formula cho serious apology: (1) REQUEST moment '잠깐 시간 좀 있어요? 사과드릴 일이 있어요' — không spring on them; (2) FACE-TO-FACE preferred — text/KaTalk apology cho serious matter = thiếu sincerity; (3) NAME specific harm — '~한 게 정말 잘못이었어요' — không '제가 잘못했어요' generic; (4) NO EXCUSES — không 'but', 'because', 'at that time'; (5) TAKE RESPONSIBILITY explicitly — '제 책임이에요' / '제가 무신경했어요'; (6) CONCRETE REPAIR — không chỉ 'I won't do it again' verbal, mà show specific action (calendar reminder, follow-up plan, time/effort to make right). Lỗi 5 năm bạn = không 30 giây apology fix; expect 1-2 weeks of demonstrated change. Hàn measure sincerity by FOLLOW-THROUGH not WORDS. Sau apology accepted: (a) DON'T bring up again unless họ initiate; (b) DO follow through on repair commitment 100%; (c) DO maintain normal contact — không withdraw out of guilt. Việt habit của 'still feeling bad weeks later, distance ourselves' bị Hàn coi là withdrawal of friendship — opposite of what they want. Show up normally + small kind gesture (coffee invite, helping with their thing) = real repair.",
      "tip_advice_en": "A six-step formula for a serious apology: (1) REQUEST a moment, '잠깐 시간 좀 있어요? 사과드릴 일이 있어요' — don't spring it on them; (2) FACE-TO-FACE preferred — a text/KakaoTalk apology for a serious matter = lacks sincerity; (3) NAME the specific harm — '~한 게 정말 잘못이었어요' (doing ~ was truly wrong) — not a generic '제가 잘못했어요' (I was wrong); (4) NO EXCUSES — no 'but', 'because', 'at that time'; (5) TAKE RESPONSIBILITY explicitly — '제 책임이에요' (it's my responsibility) / '제가 무신경했어요' (I was thoughtless); (6) CONCRETE REPAIR — not just a verbal 'I won't do it again', but a specific action (a calendar reminder, a follow-up plan, time/effort to make it right). A five-year friendship's hurt = not fixed by a 30-second apology; expect 1–2 weeks of demonstrated change. Koreans measure sincerity by FOLLOW-THROUGH, not WORDS. After the apology is accepted: (a) DON'T bring it up again unless they initiate; (b) DO follow through on the repair commitment 100%; (c) DO maintain normal contact — don't withdraw out of guilt. The Vietnamese habit of 'still feeling bad weeks later, distancing ourselves' is read by Koreans as a withdrawal of friendship — the opposite of what they want. Show up normally + a small kind gesture (a coffee invite, helping with their thing) = real repair.",
