      "cultural_notes_vi": "Korean farewell culture đặc trưng vì 정 (jeong) — quan hệ Hàn one xây qua thời gian, có ràng buộc cảm xúc. Vì thế goodbye trong Hàn nặng hơn nhiều cultures: (1) Korean coi tạm biệt = small death; bài hát Hàn-pop nhiều bài về goodbye; (2) Long-distance Korean friendships maintain rigorous contact: weekly KaTalk, monthly call, yearly visit nếu possible; (3) Once 정 formed + person moves away, returns to Korea (even briefly years later) = obligated to schedule meal với them — không reconnecting = breaks 의리; (4) Funeral attendance — even for friend's relative bạn không biết — counts strongly cho 정 banking; (5) Online presence: keep liking Instagram posts, occasional KaTalk birthday messages = maintains thread. Sai lầm Vietnamese learners: (a) Goodbye quá emotional ở boarding gate (long crying + dramatic — Hàn ưa concise + dignified in public); (b) 'Tôi sẽ không quên anh' (I will never forget you — promises feel performative); (c) Stop contact sau 6 months 'because life got busy' — Hàn coi là 의리 abandoned; (d) Reactivate contact only when need favor — broken trust. Quan trọng: small consistent contact (1 message/tuần for 6 tháng) > large performative goodbye + ghosting.",
      "cultural_notes_en": "Korean farewell culture is distinctive because of 정 (jeong) — a Korean relationship is built over time and emotionally binding. So goodbye in Korea is heavier than in many cultures: (1) Koreans treat a farewell as a small death; many K-pop songs are about goodbye; (2) long-distance Korean friendships maintain rigorous contact: weekly KakaoTalk, a monthly call, a yearly visit if possible; (3) once 정 is formed + a person moves away, returning to Korea (even briefly years later) = obliged to schedule a meal with them — not reconnecting = breaks 의리 (loyalty); (4) funeral attendance — even for a friend's relative you don't know — counts strongly toward 'jeong banking'; (5) online presence: keep liking Instagram posts, occasional KakaoTalk birthday messages = maintains the thread. Vietnamese-learner mistakes: (a) an over-emotional goodbye at the boarding gate (long crying + dramatic — Koreans prefer concise + dignified in public); (b) 'I will never forget you' (promises feel performative); (c) stopping contact after 6 months 'because life got busy' — Koreans read it as 의리 abandoned; (d) reactivating contact only when you need a favor — broken trust. Important: small consistent contact (1 message/week for 6 months) > a large performative goodbye + ghosting.",
      "tip_advice_vi": "Goodbye well structure: (1) ACKNOWLEDGE the relationship specifically — what you got from them, what they gave you. Việt-style 'cảm ơn vì tất cả' generic không đủ; nêu 1-2 cụ thể; (2) NAME the feeling — Hàn appreciate emotional honesty in private moment ('정이 많이 들었어요' — nói thẳng); (3) PROMISE concrete continuation — không 'we'll keep in touch' generic; mà 'I'll text every Sunday', 'I'll visit within 1 year' specific; (4) GIVE them something to take — small gift if appropriate, photograph, written letter (Hàn appreciate physical mementos); (5) END quickly — Việt prolong goodbye with 'ok one more thing'; Hàn ưa clean exit. Sau goodbye: (a) Day 1: send text confirming arrival ('잘 도착했어요?' — nếu họ đi); (b) Week 1-4: every 2-3 days light contact (photo, observation, life update); (c) Month 1+: sustainable rhythm 1-2x/week. (d) DO show up if họ có need (work crisis, family loss) — even from distance, sending care basket / wire money / book ticket = real 의리. Korean friendships from afar work IF effort sustained. End-of-relationship goodbye (break-up): different. Clean closure preferred — không 'maybe in future'. '우리 잘 지냈어요. 헤어지지만 서로 잘 살아요' — clean, dignified.",
      "tip_advice_en": "Saying goodbye well, structured: (1) ACKNOWLEDGE the relationship specifically — what you got from them, what they gave you. A Vietnamese-style generic 'thanks for everything' isn't enough; name 1–2 specifics; (2) NAME the feeling — Koreans appreciate emotional honesty in a private moment ('정이 많이 들었어요' — I've grown very attached, said plainly); (3) PROMISE a concrete continuation — not a generic 'we'll keep in touch', but 'I'll text every Sunday', 'I'll visit within 1 year' specific; (4) GIVE them something to take — a small gift if appropriate, a photograph, a written letter (Koreans appreciate physical mementos); (5) END quickly — Vietnamese prolong goodbyes with 'ok one more thing'; Koreans prefer a clean exit. After the goodbye: (a) Day 1: send a text confirming arrival ('잘 도착했어요?' — if they traveled); (b) Weeks 1–4: light contact every 2–3 days (a photo, an observation, a life update); (c) Month 1+: a sustainable rhythm 1–2x/week; (d) DO show up if they have a need (a work crisis, a family loss) — even from a distance, sending a care basket / wiring money / booking a ticket = real 의리. Korean friendships from afar work IF effort is sustained. An end-of-relationship goodbye (a break-up) is different: clean closure is preferred — no 'maybe in the future'. '우리 잘 지냈어요. 헤어지지만 서로 잘 살아요' (we had a good time; we're parting but let's each live well) — clean, dignified.",
