      "cultural_notes_vi": "Korean stress culture rất particular: (1) 표면 (surface) culture — Hàn thường downplay stress publicly; '괜찮아요' default response even when not OK. Bạn Việt cần đọc visual cues: tired face, weight loss, withdrawal from group, missed events; (2) Mental health stigma vẫn còn ở Hàn (đặc biệt với 30+ generation) — mention 'therapy', 'counseling' có thể uncomfortable. Use 'talking it out' framing thay vì 'professional help' framing in early conversations; (3) Friendship support gestures Korean style: (a) Sending food (Korean delivery culture — gửi 죽 cháo, đồ bổ qua Coupang Eats — không cần explain); (b) Just sitting nearby in silence — Hàn coi presence as comfort; (c) Specific practical help (ride, errand, cooking for them) — better than vague 'let me know if you need anything'; (4) 회식 với close friends khi stress — Hàn culture coi shared meal là natural healing space; (5) Family-related stress đặc biệt heavy in Korean culture vì Confucian filial obligation — friends being supportive về parental caregiving = real friendship. Tránh: (a) Posting about their stress on social media; (b) Triangulating with mutual friends ('A told me B is depressed'); (c) Bringing up their stress at group settings — private only. (6) Khi bạn là người mới ở Hàn, sometimes Korean friends share stress với bạn precisely vì bạn outsider không gossip in their network — privilege that requires honoring.",
      "cultural_notes_en": "Korean stress culture is very particular: (1) a 표면 (surface) culture — Koreans usually downplay stress publicly; '괜찮아요' (I'm fine) is the default even when not OK. A Vietnamese friend needs to read visual cues: a tired face, weight loss, withdrawal from the group, missed events; (2) mental-health stigma still exists in Korea (especially with the 30+ generation) — mentioning 'therapy', 'counseling' can be uncomfortable. Use a 'talking it out' framing rather than a 'professional help' framing in early conversations; (3) Korean-style friendship support gestures: (a) sending food (Korean delivery culture — send 죽 porridge or restorative food via Coupang Eats — no need to explain); (b) just sitting nearby in silence — Koreans treat presence as comfort; (c) specific practical help (a ride, an errand, cooking for them) — better than a vague 'let me know if you need anything'; (4) 회식 with close friends when stressed — Korean culture treats a shared meal as a natural healing space; (5) family-related stress is especially heavy in Korean culture because of the Confucian filial obligation — friends being supportive about parental caregiving = real friendship. Avoid: (a) posting about their stress on social media; (b) triangulating with mutual friends ('A told me B is depressed'); (c) bringing up their stress in a group setting — private only. (6) When you're new in Korea, sometimes Korean friends share stress with you precisely because you're an outsider who won't gossip in their network — a privilege that requires honoring.",
      "tip_advice_vi": "Listen-first formula 5 bước: (1) ASK don't ASSUME — '어떤 마음인지 얘기해 줄 수 있어요?' (open-ended) > '왜 슬퍼요?' (probing); (2) VALIDATE before exploring — '많이 힘들겠어요' before 'tell me what happened'; (3) REFLECT what you heard — restate their feelings cho show you absorbed: '그 외로움 정말 무거워요'; (4) ASK what they need — '내가 뭐 도와줄 수 있어요?' không assume; (5) OFFER specific not generic — '다음 주 화요일 같이 갈게요' > '도움이 필요하면 말해요'. Bonus rules: (a) DON'T try fix in first conversation; (b) DON'T compare to your stress; (c) DON'T overshare advice unless họ explicitly ask 'what should I do?'; (d) DO follow up — 1 tuần sau 'X 지난번 얘기한 거 어떻게 됐어요?' shows you remembered; (e) DO show up consistently — Korean stress trauma từ flaky support people. Khi đề xuất practical help: BE SPECIFIC + TIME-BOUND. 'Tôi đi cùng anh đưa mẹ đi viện thứ Ba tuần sau' > 'Tôi sẽ giúp anh khi cần'. Concrete commitment = real support trong Korean culture. Sau buổi nói chuyện: gửi short message check-in 24h sau ('어제 잘 들어가셨어요? 마음 좀 어때요?') — sustained care = trust building. Long-term: Korean friend đã share vulnerability với bạn = stronger bond formed. Honor it với consistent presence — nhỏ thôi nhưng đều.",
      "tip_advice_en": "A listen-first formula, 5 steps: (1) ASK don't ASSUME — '어떤 마음인지 얘기해 줄 수 있어요?' (can you tell me how you're feeling?, open-ended) > '왜 슬퍼요?' (why are you sad?, probing); (2) VALIDATE before exploring — '많이 힘들겠어요' (that must be so hard) before 'tell me what happened'; (3) REFLECT what you heard — restate their feelings to show you absorbed it: '그 외로움 정말 무거워요' (that loneliness is really heavy); (4) ASK what they need — '내가 뭐 도와줄 수 있어요?' don't assume; (5) OFFER specific not generic — '다음 주 화요일 같이 갈게요' (I'll go with you next Tuesday) > '도움이 필요하면 말해요' (tell me if you need help). Bonus rules: (a) DON'T try to fix it in the first conversation; (b) DON'T compare it to your own stress; (c) DON'T over-advise unless they explicitly ask 'what should I do?'; (d) DO follow up — a week later '지난번 얘기한 거 어떻게 됐어요?' (how did that thing we talked about go?) shows you remembered; (e) DO show up consistently — Korean stress trauma comes from flaky support people. When offering practical help: BE SPECIFIC + TIME-BOUND. 'I'll go with you to take your mom to the hospital next Tuesday' > 'I'll help when you need it'. A concrete commitment = real support in Korean culture. After the conversation: send a short check-in message 24h later ('어제 잘 들어가셨어요? 마음 좀 어때요?' — did you get home okay? how are you feeling?) — sustained care = trust building. Long-term: a Korean friend who shared vulnerability with you = a stronger bond formed. Honor it with consistent presence — small but regular.",
