      "cultural_notes_vi": "Korean money culture vs Việt: (1) Việt khá thoải mái với 'borrowing tiền giữa bạn bè' — cho mượn small amounts thường xuyên, không tracking strictly. Hàn opposite — coi money loans giữa friends là risky for relationship; default strategy là KHÔNG mượn / cho mượn; (2) When Hàn DOES borrow giữa bạn bè, expect repayment within stated timeframe NO EXCEPTIONS. Late = serious 의리 violation; (3) Korean payment habits: 분할결제 (split bill) common — ai ăn gì người đó trả, hoặc rotation 'hôm nay tôi mời, lần sau bạn mời' — informal balance. Borrowing chỉ khi true emergency; (4) Hàn strong tradition giúp bạn TRONG NGOẠI TRỪ tiền — drive bạn somewhere, take time off work to visit, cook, run errand. Money lending coi là 'crossing line' that turns friendship into business. (5) When borrowing happens between friends: (a) Specific amount written down (KaTalk message OK as record); (b) Specific due date stated; (c) Repayment với KaTalk Pay or bank transfer (not cash — record); (d) Repayment + small extra (커피값 5천 원, lunch invite) — not interest, but 'thanks for trust' gesture. (6) Vietnamese learners common mistakes: assume Korean friend will be casual khi borrowing, không formalize → friend feels betrayed when 'small amount' kéo dài; assume they'll give without asking → over-borrowing accidentally. Quy tắc vàng: borrow chỉ for true emergency, repay early or on-time, never let it lapse.",
      "cultural_notes_en": "Korean money culture vs Vietnamese: (1) Vietnamese are fairly relaxed about 'borrowing money between friends' — lending small amounts often, not tracking strictly. Korea is the opposite — money loans between friends are seen as risky for the relationship; the default strategy is NOT to borrow / lend; (2) when a Korean DOES borrow between friends, repayment within the stated timeframe is expected, NO EXCEPTIONS. Late = a serious 의리 violation; (3) Korean payment habits: 분할결제 (split bill) is common — each pays for what they had, or a rotation 'today I treat, next time you treat' — an informal balance. Borrowing only in a true emergency; (4) Koreans strongly help friends with EVERYTHING EXCEPT money — drive you somewhere, take time off to visit, cook, run an errand. Money lending is seen as 'crossing a line' that turns friendship into business. (5) When borrowing does happen between friends: (a) a specific amount written down (a KakaoTalk message works as a record); (b) a specific due date stated; (c) repayment via KakaoTalk Pay or bank transfer (not cash — a record); (d) repayment + a small extra (₩5,000 coffee, a lunch invite) — not interest, but a 'thanks for trusting me' gesture. (6) Common Vietnamese-learner mistakes: assuming a Korean friend will be casual about borrowing, not formalizing → the friend feels betrayed when a 'small amount' drags on; assuming they'll give without asking → accidental over-borrowing. Golden rule: borrow only for a true emergency, repay early or on-time, never let it lapse.",
      "tip_advice_vi": "Three-phase approach cho money topic: PREVENT (ideal): (1) When ăn cùng, alternate paying or split bill clearly; (2) Don't borrow casually — cover small amounts yourself, save lending for emergencies; (3) Khi help bạn financially, prefer GIFT over LOAN — '이건 선물이에요' (it's a gift) eliminates repayment ambiguity. ASK (when borrowing): (1) Acknowledge awkwardness — '정말 부탁하기 어려운 얘기인데요'; (2) Be specific about amount, purpose, repayment date; (3) Write IOU as KaTalk message (text record); (4) Pay early hoặc on-time + small extra (curry coffee, lunch invite). RESOLVE (when collecting late debt): (1) Wait reasonable period (1 month past due before bringing up); (2) Private conversation, không group; (3) Open với honest acknowledgment '좀 어색한 주제'; (4) Specific reference (date + amount); (5) Offer flexibility (천천히 갚아도 돼요) but firm date; (6) After resolution, CLEAN SLATE — không bring up again. Worst-case write-off rule: nếu sau 12 tháng + 2-3 attempts collecting still no payment, decide internally: write off (treat as gift) hoặc end friendship. Korean culture: most friends pay back; những who don't = signaling unwillingness, time to step back from financial entanglement. Khi từ chối mượn: ngắn + clear + không over-explain. '죄송하지만 이번에는 빌려드리기 어려워요. 제 사정이 좀 그래요' = polite + firm. Hàn appreciate honesty over false-yes. Restore quan hệ qua next casual interaction (không apologize for refusing).",
      "tip_advice_en": "A three-phase approach to the money topic. PREVENT (ideal): (1) when eating together, alternate paying or split the bill clearly; (2) don't borrow casually — cover small amounts yourself, save lending for emergencies; (3) when helping a friend financially, prefer a GIFT over a LOAN — '이건 선물이에요' (this is a gift) eliminates repayment ambiguity. ASK (when borrowing): (1) acknowledge the awkwardness — '정말 부탁하기 어려운 얘기인데요' (this is really hard to ask); (2) be specific about amount, purpose, repayment date; (3) write the IOU as a KakaoTalk message (a text record); (4) pay early or on-time + a small extra (a coffee, a lunch invite). RESOLVE (collecting a late debt): (1) wait a reasonable period (1 month past due before bringing it up); (2) a private conversation, not in a group; (3) open with an honest acknowledgment '좀 어색한 주제' (a slightly awkward subject); (4) a specific reference (date + amount); (5) offer flexibility ('천천히 갚아도 돼요' — you can repay slowly) but a firm date; (6) after resolution, CLEAN SLATE — don't bring it up again. Worst-case write-off rule: if after 12 months + 2–3 collection attempts there's still no payment, decide internally: write it off (treat it as a gift) or end the friendship. Korean culture: most friends pay back; those who don't are signaling unwillingness — time to step back from financial entanglement. When refusing to lend: short + clear + don't over-explain. '죄송하지만 이번에는 빌려드리기 어려워요. 제 사정이 좀 그래요' (sorry, I can't lend this time; my situation is a bit tight) = polite + firm. Koreans appreciate honesty over a false yes. Restore the relationship through the next casual interaction (don't apologize for refusing).",
