      "cultural_notes_vi": "Korean religious landscape thực tế: (1) Census ~30% Christian (Protestant ~20%, Catholic ~10%), ~20% Buddhist, ~50% 무교 (no religion). Geographic skew: Seoul/Gyeonggi more Christian; rural / 영남 region more Buddhist. (2) Korean Christianity history: missionaries arrived 1880s; church boom post-Korean War (1950s+) tied to social services + rebuilding; some megachurches (수만 명 attendance) prominent. Active proselytizing tradition exists in some Korean churches — historical context. Modern younger generation Korean Christians often more low-key. (3) Korean Buddhism: 조계종 dominant lineage; many temples (Bulguksa, Jogyesa). Cultural Buddhist (절 visits at New Year, 부처님 오신 날) common even among 'no religion' Koreans. (4) 무교 status không carries social stigma in Korea unlike some places — neutral default. (5) Cross-religious family tolerance high — within same family, members of different faiths is normal, not source of conflict. (6) Workplace religious expression: low — not common to discuss faith at work; 회식 secular by default. (7) Vietnamese cultural framing: (a) Việt also has Christianity (~7% Catholic concentrated), Buddhism (~12% practicing, more cultural), majority cultural ancestor veneration + folk religion mix. Not exact Korean parallel but related; (b) Khi explain to Korean: 'ancestor veneration' = 조상 숭배 (precise Korean term) — Hàn understand quickly. Avoid 'I worship my grandfather' (sounds like deification — Christian Korean may misunderstand); say 'I honor and remember' (기리다 / 기억하다). (8) Vietnamese learners common mistake: assume Korean Christian friends will judge other faiths — most Korean Christians today private about own faith, không pushy. Some are; majority not.",
      "cultural_notes_en": "Korea's actual religious landscape: (1) census ~30% Christian (Protestant ~20%, Catholic ~10%), ~20% Buddhist, ~50% 무교 (no religion). Geographic skew: Seoul/Gyeonggi more Christian; rural / Yeongnam region more Buddhist. (2) Korean Christianity history: missionaries arrived in the 1880s; a church boom post-Korean War (1950s+) tied to social services + rebuilding; some megachurches (tens of thousands of attendees) are prominent. An active proselytizing tradition exists in some Korean churches — historical context. The modern younger generation of Korean Christians is often more low-key. (3) Korean Buddhism: the 조계종 (Jogye Order) is the dominant lineage; many temples (Bulguksa, Jogyesa). Cultural Buddhism (temple visits at New Year, 부처님 오신 날 / Buddha's Birthday) is common even among 'no religion' Koreans. (4) 무교 (no-religion) status carries no social stigma in Korea, unlike some places — a neutral default. (5) Cross-religious family tolerance is high — within one family, members of different faiths is normal, not a source of conflict. (6) Workplace religious expression: low — not common to discuss faith at work; 회식 is secular by default. (7) Vietnamese cultural framing: (a) Vietnam also has Christianity (~7% Catholic, concentrated), Buddhism (~12% practicing, more cultural), and a majority cultural ancestor-veneration + folk-religion mix. Not an exact Korean parallel but related; (b) when explaining to a Korean: 'ancestor veneration' = 조상 숭배 (the precise Korean term) — Koreans understand it quickly. Avoid 'I worship my grandfather' (sounds like deification — a Christian Korean may misunderstand); say 'I honor and remember' (기리다 / 기억하다). (8) Common Vietnamese-learner mistake: assuming Korean Christian friends will judge other faiths — most Korean Christians today are private about their own faith, not pushy. Some are; the majority aren't.",
      "tip_advice_vi": "Faith conversation 5 nguyên tắc: (1) ASK don't ASSUME — '혹시 종교 갖고 계세요?' before sharing yours; (2) SHARE briefly when asked — không lecture, 1-2 sentences; (3) RESPECT silence — nếu họ skip topic, don't push; (4) DECLINE invite politely — '관심 가져 주셔서 감사하지만 제 길은 다른 곳이에요. 마음만 받을게요'; (5) FIND common ground — even across faiths, shared values (family, kindness, ethics) bridge. Specific situations: (a) Khi họ pray before meal: bow head silent (not pray); 음식에 감사 (thanks for food) — secular form; (b) Khi mời go church: 'thanks but I'll find my own path' — clear decline; (c) Khi họ struggling và pray, listen + supportive without engaging theology; (d) Khi họ mock other faiths: gentle pushback '저는 다양성이 좋다고 생각해요' — disagree without lecture. Sai lầm Vietnamese learners: (a) Over-share Vietnamese folk religion details (too unfamiliar to Korean ear, lose audience); (b) Compare 'Christianity stricter than Buddhism' (avoid ranking); (c) Convert when invited because pressured (do because want, không pressure); (d) Hide faith identity to avoid awkward (Hàn appreciate honest 'I'm X / no religion' over evasion). Long-term cross-faith friendship: works fine khi both respect 각자 길. Yearly check-in: 'X 종교 행사 있어요?' shows interest without pushing. // TODO native review: '관심 가져 주셔서 감사해요' as decline phrase — verify softness vs alternatives.",
      "tip_advice_en": "Five principles for a faith conversation: (1) ASK don't ASSUME — '혹시 종교 갖고 계세요?' (do you have a religion?) before sharing yours; (2) SHARE briefly when asked — don't lecture, 1–2 sentences; (3) RESPECT silence — if they skip the topic, don't push; (4) DECLINE an invite politely — '관심 가져 주셔서 감사하지만 제 길은 다른 곳이에요. 마음만 받을게요' (thank you for the interest, but my path is elsewhere; I'll just accept the kindness); (5) FIND common ground — even across faiths, shared values (family, kindness, ethics) bridge it. Specific situations: (a) when they pray before a meal: bow your head silently (don't pray); '음식에 감사' (thanks for the food) — a secular form; (b) when invited to church: 'thanks but I'll find my own path' — a clear decline; (c) when they're struggling and praying, listen + be supportive without engaging the theology; (d) when they mock other faiths: a gentle pushback '저는 다양성이 좋다고 생각해요' (I think diversity is good) — disagree without lecturing. Vietnamese-learner mistakes: (a) over-sharing Vietnamese folk-religion details (too unfamiliar to a Korean ear, you lose the audience); (b) comparing 'Christianity is stricter than Buddhism' (avoid ranking); (c) converting when invited because pressured (do it because you want to, not under pressure); (d) hiding your faith identity to avoid awkwardness (Koreans appreciate an honest 'I'm X / no religion' over evasion). A long-term cross-faith friendship works fine when both respect 각자 길 (each one's own path). A yearly check-in: 'any X religious events coming up?' shows interest without pushing. // TODO native review: '관심 가져 주셔서 감사해요' as a decline phrase — verify softness vs alternatives.",
