      "cultural_notes_vi": "Korean culture về sharing personal struggle: (1) Default privacy — Hàn culture historically save face; family difficulty, mental health, past mistakes thường buried. Sharing = significant trust act, not casual; (2) Foreign friend privilege — bạn ngoài Hàn society network thường được trusted với những stories Korean friend won't tell other Koreans (no fear gossip ricochet through 학연/지연 networks); (3) Once shared, NEVER bring up unless họ initiate. Silence là cách Hàn honor the moment. Asking follow-up questions days later = pressuring; (4) Don't relay even to your spouse (unless họ explicitly OK) — cross-cultural friend may not realize Korean expectation is absolute confidentiality; (5) Common topics in vulnerability share: parental divorce / death (still stigmatized in older Korean cohorts), mental health struggle, academic failure (특히 Suneung 수능 underperformance — major life-defining moment in Korean culture), past relationship trauma, financial hardship in family, military service trauma (남자), career setback. (6) Non-Korean reaction Vietnamese learners may default to: solving, comparing own pain, 'it's normal!' minimizing — all read as not-honoring the trust. Korean expectation: just sit with them; nodding, 'thank you for telling me', occasional clarification question. (7) Long-term: friendships post-vulnerability share = much deeper. Honor trust = Korean friend will share more over time, support you back, be loyal in ways beyond average friendship. (8) If you (Vietnamese learner) want to share own vulnerability với Korean friend, watch their reaction first — some Korean friends not equipped to receive (cultural barrier), better to share with Vietnamese friends or therapist; some Korean friends do well — calibrate.",
      "cultural_notes_en": "Korean culture about sharing personal struggle: (1) Default privacy — Korean culture historically saves face; family difficulty, mental health, past mistakes are usually buried. Sharing = a significant trust act, not casual; (2) Foreign-friend privilege — being outside the Korean society network, you're often trusted with stories a Korean friend won't tell other Koreans (no fear of gossip ricocheting through the 학연/지연 networks); (3) Once shared, NEVER bring it up unless they initiate. Silence is how Koreans honor the moment. Asking follow-up questions days later = pressuring; (4) don't relay it even to your spouse (unless they explicitly OK it) — a cross-cultural friend may not realize the Korean expectation is absolute confidentiality; (5) common vulnerability-share topics: parental divorce / death (still stigmatized in older Korean cohorts), a mental-health struggle, academic failure (especially Suneung 수능 underperformance — a major life-defining moment in Korean culture), past relationship trauma, family financial hardship, military-service trauma (for men), a career setback. (6) Default non-Korean reactions Vietnamese learners may have: solving, comparing your own pain, 'it's normal!' minimizing — all read as not honoring the trust. The Korean expectation: just sit with them; nodding, 'thank you for telling me', an occasional clarifying question. (7) Long-term: friendships post-vulnerability-share = much deeper. Honoring the trust = the Korean friend will share more over time, support you back, be loyal beyond average friendship. (8) If you (the Vietnamese learner) want to share your own vulnerability with a Korean friend, watch their reaction first — some Korean friends aren't equipped to receive it (a cultural barrier); better to share with Vietnamese friends or a therapist; some Korean friends do well — calibrate.",
      "tip_advice_vi": "Listener checklist 8 items: (1) PUT PHONE AWAY — visibly setting aside signals fully present; (2) DON'T FILL SILENCE — let pause sit. Korean speaker will continue khi ready; (3) MIRROR feeling not facts — '그 시간 외로웠을 거예요' (must have been lonely) > '아, 9살부터 그랬군요' (ah, since age 9); (4) ASK to humanize past — '그때 본인은 어땠어요?' (how were you then?) — gives permission to talk about self-then; (5) THANK explicitly — '얘기해 줘서 고마워요' — verbalize trust received; (6) DON'T COMPARE own pain even if relatable — wait until other moment; (7) COMMIT confidentiality VERBALLY — '우리 둘 사이에서만 있을 거예요'; (8) LET them stop when ready — không probe '그래서 그 다음에는?'. After conversation: (a) Send brief KaTalk that night '오늘 시간 가져 줘서 고마워요. 잘 자요' — reaffirms care without re-opening topic; (b) Don't bring up next time unless họ do; (c) DO show up consistently — friend who heard hard truth + still showing up normally = healing presence. Long-term: small actions matter — remembered detail mention naturally ('지난번 얘기한 동생 잘 지내요?' — only nếu safe), thoughtful gift on relevant date (mother's death anniversary if you knew). Việt learner advantage in Korean friendship: outsider position lets them be safer container for Korean vulnerability than Korean friends inside same network.",
      "tip_advice_en": "A listener checklist, 8 items: (1) PUT THE PHONE AWAY — visibly setting it aside signals fully present; (2) DON'T FILL SILENCE — let the pause sit. The Korean speaker continues when ready; (3) MIRROR the feeling, not the facts — '그 시간 외로웠을 거예요' (you must have been lonely then) > '아, 9살부터 그랬군요' (ah, since age 9); (4) ASK to humanize the past — '그때 본인은 어땠어요?' (how were you then?) — gives permission to talk about their then-self; (5) THANK explicitly — '얘기해 줘서 고마워요' (thank you for telling me) — verbalize the trust received; (6) DON'T COMPARE your own pain even if relatable — wait for another moment; (7) COMMIT confidentiality VERBALLY — '우리 둘 사이에서만 있을 거예요' (this stays just between us); (8) LET them stop when ready — don't probe '그래서 그 다음에는?' (so what happened next?). After the conversation: (a) send a brief KakaoTalk that night '오늘 시간 가져 줘서 고마워요. 잘 자요' (thank you for the time today; sleep well) — reaffirms care without re-opening the topic; (b) don't bring it up next time unless they do; (c) DO show up consistently — a friend who heard a hard truth + still shows up normally = a healing presence. Long-term: small actions matter — naturally mentioning a remembered detail ('지난번 얘기한 동생 잘 지내요?' — how's the sibling you mentioned? only if safe), a thoughtful gift on a relevant date (a mother's death anniversary if you knew). The Vietnamese-learner advantage in a Korean friendship: the outsider position lets you be a safer container for Korean vulnerability than Korean friends inside the same network.",
