      "cultural_notes_vi": "Korean friendship intensity: (1) Korean close friendships often more demanding than Western/Vietnamese norm — daily contact, frequent meetings, sharing personal updates expected as 의리 evidence. Một số Korean friends measure closeness by frequency contact; (2) Vietnamese learners often overcommit early to demonstrate 정 / commitment, then burn out 6-12 months in. Better: pace yourself from start, sustainable rhythm; (3) Korean concept '눈치' — picking up social cues. Hàn expect bạn read tones, không demand explicit boundary statements. Việt learner accustomed to direct request culture (Việt is less indirect than Hàn) needs balance: Hàn appreciate framing softer than Việt comfort; (4) Setting boundary EARLY in friendship (first 6 months) easier than fixing later — cultural mismatch crystallizes into resentment if not addressed. (5) Common Vietnamese-Korean friendship boundaries needed: (a) Time — Hàn workaholics may not realize bạn cần personal time; (b) Information — Hàn family ask intrusive questions (lương, marriage, weight) considered normal between Korean intimates; (c) Communication frequency — KakaoTalk culture intense; (d) Money entanglement (covered in lesson 88); (e) Drinking — Hàn 회식 culture pressures attendance; saying no without giving up entire friendship requires skill. (6) Healthy friendship signal: friend respects your no without sulking long-term. If their reaction is dramatic withdrawal sau boundary setting, that's information về relationship sustainability. (7) Long-term Vietnamese-Korean friendships work best với explicit conversation về expectations early — '우리 일주일에 한 번 만나는 정도 좋아요?' (once a week good?) — set rhythm explicitly.",
      "cultural_notes_en": "Korean friendship intensity: (1) Korean close friendships are often more demanding than the Western/Vietnamese norm — daily contact, frequent meetings, sharing personal updates expected as evidence of 의리. Some Korean friends measure closeness by contact frequency; (2) Vietnamese learners often overcommit early to demonstrate 정 / commitment, then burn out 6–12 months in. Better: pace yourself from the start, a sustainable rhythm; (3) the Korean concept '눈치' — picking up social cues. Koreans expect you to read tones, not demand explicit boundary statements. The Vietnamese learner, used to a more direct-request culture (Vietnamese is less indirect than Korean), needs balance: Koreans appreciate framing softer than the Vietnamese comfort level; (4) setting a boundary EARLY in the friendship (first 6 months) is easier than fixing it later — a cultural mismatch crystallizes into resentment if not addressed. (5) Common Vietnamese–Korean friendship boundaries needed: (a) Time — Korean workaholics may not realize you need personal time; (b) Information — Korean families ask intrusive questions (salary, marriage, weight) considered normal between Korean intimates; (c) Communication frequency — KakaoTalk culture is intense; (d) money entanglement (covered in lesson 88); (e) Drinking — Korean 회식 culture pressures attendance; saying no without giving up the whole friendship requires skill. (6) Healthy-friendship signal: the friend respects your no without long-term sulking. If their reaction is a dramatic withdrawal after you set a boundary, that's information about the relationship's sustainability. (7) Long-term Vietnamese–Korean friendships work best with an explicit conversation about expectations early — '우리 일주일에 한 번 만나는 정도 좋아요?' (is meeting once a week good?) — set the rhythm explicitly.",
      "tip_advice_vi": "Boundary-setting framework 5 steps: (1) PERMISSION request — '솔직하게 말씀드려도 돼요?' Hàn appreciate respect of being asked first; (2) FRAME positive — 'for friendship', 'long-term', 'better quality'; (3) SPECIFIC concrete — exact behavior + exact limit ('11시 이후 KaTalk 자제' > 'less contact'); (4) REASON brief — one sentence why ('잠 부족해서') không paragraph; (5) ALTERNATIVE proposal — '대신 매주 토요일 만나면 어때요?' — show enhancement, không reduction. Timing: (a) Set boundary FACE-TO-FACE preferred for major issues; (b) KaTalk OK for small adjustments ('밤늦게 답 어려워서 아침에 답할게요'); (c) NOT during active issue moment; chọn neutral time; (d) NOT during họ stressful period. Watch for reaction: (a) 'Sorry, I'll change' = healthy → relationship will improve; (b) Defensive '하지만 나는…' (but I…) = needs more conversation; (c) Cold withdrawal = signal incompatible expectations, painful but information; (d) Sulking 1-2 days then return = normal Korean adjustment, không panic. Khi bị Korean friend overwhelm with TMI sharing: redirect to action — 'X에 대해서 어떻게 행동하실 거예요?' (what action will you take about X?). Listening role không infinite. Khi being asked invasive personal questions: '그 부분은 좀 사적이라' + redirect to other topic. Often. Hàn will eventually learn pattern. Long-term friendship = both adjusting; bạn không only one accommodating. Watch for reciprocity over 6 months.",
      "tip_advice_en": "A boundary-setting framework, 5 steps: (1) PERMISSION request — '솔직하게 말씀드려도 돼요?' (may I speak frankly?). Koreans appreciate being asked first; (2) FRAME it positive — 'for the friendship', 'long-term', 'better quality'; (3) SPECIFIC and concrete — the exact behavior + exact limit ('11시 이후 KaTalk 자제' — easing off KakaoTalk after 11pm > 'less contact'); (4) REASON brief — one sentence why ('잠 부족해서' — I'm short on sleep), not a paragraph; (5) ALTERNATIVE proposal — '대신 매주 토요일 만나면 어때요?' (instead, how about meeting every Saturday?) — show an enhancement, not a reduction. Timing: (a) set a boundary FACE-TO-FACE for major issues; (b) KakaoTalk is OK for small adjustments ('밤늦게 답 어려워서 아침에 답할게요' — hard to reply late, I'll reply in the morning); (c) NOT during an active issue moment; choose a neutral time; (d) NOT during their stressful period. Watch for the reaction: (a) 'Sorry, I'll change' = healthy → the relationship will improve; (b) defensive '하지만 나는…' (but I…) = needs more conversation; (c) cold withdrawal = a signal of incompatible expectations, painful but informative; (d) sulking 1–2 days then returning = a normal Korean adjustment, don't panic. When a Korean friend overwhelms you with TMI sharing: redirect to action — 'X에 대해서 어떻게 행동하실 거예요?' (what action will you take about X?). The listening role is not infinite. When asked invasive personal questions: '그 부분은 좀 사적이라' (that part is a bit private) + redirect to another topic. Often. The Korean friend will eventually learn the pattern. A long-term friendship = both adjusting; you're not the only one accommodating. Watch for reciprocity over 6 months.",
